# Lifecycle case: a money feature (trust boundary) asked for as an outcome. The prompt names no skill, stage or command.
# pass_score: fraction of the six stage artifacts (spec, spec-check, plan, ledger build events, review, ship) required.
# scored by the six AIDLC stage artifacts.
{"id": "L1-refund", "pass_score": 0.67, "prompt": "/aegisops-harness:aidlc Customers sometimes send a wallet transfer to the wrong person. Add refunds for transfers in src/wallet.js: a refund returns the money to the sender, a transfer can be refunded at most once, and never for more than was sent. Take it all the way to a change that is ready to ship. I am not available for questions, so make reasonable decisions and write them down."}
# Spec handed over in the to-spec template (the format users write with grilling + to-spec), full mode: the same
# refund feature, scored by stage artifacts; the outcome checks and doc lines are reported alongside.
{"id": "L2-refund-spec", "pass_score": 0.67, "prompt": "/aegisops-harness:aidlc Build this spec and take it all the way to a change that is ready to ship. I am not available for questions, so make reasonable decisions and write them down.\n\nStatus: ready-for-agent\n\n# Wallet transfer refunds\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nCustomers sometimes send a wallet transfer to the wrong person. Today nothing can undo a transfer, so support moves money back by hand with a new transfer, which is error-prone and can pay out twice.\n\n## Solution\n\nA transfer gets an identifier. Support can refund a transfer by that identifier: the money goes back from the recipient to the sender, a transfer is refunded at most once, and a refund is never for more than was sent.\n\n## User Stories\n\n1. As a sender, I want every transfer to have an identifier, so that a specific transfer can be refunded later.\n2. As a support agent, I want to refund a transfer by its identifier, so that the money goes back to the sender.\n3. As a sender, I want a refund to return the amount to my balance and take it from the recipient, so that both balances are as before the transfer.\n4. As a support agent, I want a second refund of the same transfer to be rejected, so that the sender is never paid twice.\n5. As a support agent, I want a refund for more than the transfer amount to be rejected, so that the wallet never pays out more than was sent.\n6. As a support agent, I want a refund of an unknown transfer to be rejected, so that a typo cannot move money.\n7. As a support agent, I want a rejected refund to leave every balance unchanged, so that a failed refund has no side effects.\n\n## Implementation Decisions\n\n- One seam: the wallet module's public functions. `transfer` returns the transfer identifier; a new `refund(transferId, amount?)` refunds the full amount when no amount is given.\n- A refund records the transfer as refunded before any balance changes, so a repeated call cannot pay twice.\n- Rejections throw an error with a stable code: `TRANSFER_NOT_FOUND`, `ALREADY_REFUNDED`, `REFUND_EXCEEDS_TRANSFER`.\n- **Security:** assets are the sender's and recipient's balances. Threats: a replayed refund (tampering), a refund above the transfer (elevation), an unknown or forged identifier (spoofing). Controls: at most one refund per transfer, amount at most the transfer amount, unknown identifiers rejected, no balance change on rejection.\n\n## Testing Decisions\n\n- Test only through the wallet module's public functions (deposit, transfer, refund, balance); assert balances and errors, never internal state.\n- Good tests: full refund restores both balances; a second refund is rejected; an amount above the transfer is rejected; an unknown identifier is rejected; each rejection leaves balances unchanged.\n- Prior art: the existing transfer test in test/wallet.test.js.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Partial refunds of the remaining amount after a first partial refund.\n- Persistence: balances stay in memory.\n- Changing balances from floating point to integer minor units.\n\n## Further Notes\n\n- Balances are JavaScript numbers; the existing behaviour is kept.\n"}
# Delegated spec with a national ID in events and no Security section (the auth-svc report): the security review
# finds open decisions (PII in events, compliance regime). Pass also needs zero AskUserQuestion calls (attempts count).
{"id": "L3-delegated-pii", "pass_score": 0.67, "max_questions": 0, "allowed_tools_extra": ["AskUserQuestion"], "prompt": "/aegisops-harness:aidlc Build this spec end to end, do not ask: take your recommended default for any open decision, record it, and list it in the final report.\n\nStatus: ready-for-agent\n\n# Wallet transfer refunds with an audit event\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nCustomers sometimes send a wallet transfer to the wrong person. Support moves money back by hand, and the risk team has no record of it.\n\n## Solution\n\nA transfer gets an identifier. Support can refund a transfer by that identifier, at most once and never for more than was sent. Every refund publishes an audit event for the risk team in the existing audit format, which identifies the customer by their national ID number.\n\n## User Stories\n\n1. As a sender, I want every transfer to have an identifier, so that a specific transfer can be refunded later.\n2. As a support agent, I want to refund a transfer by its identifier, so that the money goes back to the sender.\n3. As a support agent, I want a second refund of the same transfer to be rejected, so that the sender is never paid twice.\n4. As a support agent, I want a refund for more than the transfer amount to be rejected, so that the wallet never pays out more than was sent.\n5. As a support agent, I want a refund of an unknown transfer to be rejected, so that a typo cannot move money.\n6. As a risk analyst, I want every successful refund to publish an audit event with the transfer, the amount and the customer, so that refunds can be checked for fraud.\n\n## Implementation Decisions\n\n- One seam: the wallet module's public functions. `transfer` returns the transfer identifier; a new `refund(transferId, amount?)` refunds the full amount when no amount is given.\n- Audit events go to a new `onAudit(listener)` subscription in the wallet module, in the existing audit format: `{ type, transferId, amount, customer }`, where customer is the sender's national ID number.\n- Rejections throw an error with a stable code: `TRANSFER_NOT_FOUND`, `ALREADY_REFUNDED`, `REFUND_EXCEEDS_TRANSFER`.\n\n## Testing Decisions\n\n- Test only through the wallet module's public functions; assert balances, errors and published events.\n- Prior art: the existing transfer test in test/wallet.test.js.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Persistence: balances stay in memory.\n\n## Further Notes\n\n- The national ID number of a user is passed to `deposit(user, amount, nationalId)` as an optional third argument.\n"}
# Harder cases (6.0.0). The refund cases above are one small feature on an 8-line file, where the path without
# a spec already scored every outcome check; these three are where a session has to read, plan and test.
# Brownfield: a fee across three modules of an existing code base (ledger, wallet, statement) whose tests must be
# updated where the fee changes a balance. Hidden checks: rounding boundaries, atomic rejection, statement line.
{"id": "L4-brownfield-fee", "fixture": "brownfield", "hidden": "hidden_fee.mjs", "pass_score": 0.67, "hidden_checks": ["fee_quote", "sender_pays_amount_plus_fee", "conserved", "insufficient_is_atomic", "statement_has_fee_line", "api_unchanged"], "prompt": "/aegisops-harness:aidlc Build this spec and take it all the way to a change that is ready to ship. I am not available for questions, so make reasonable decisions and write them down.\n\nStatus: ready-for-agent\n\n# Transfer fee\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nTransfers between wallet accounts are free. Finance wants a fee on every transfer, and support wants customers to see it on their statement instead of a smaller amount arriving.\n\n## Solution\n\nEvery transfer charges the sender a fee of 1% of the amount on top of the amount. The fee goes to a fee account and shows as its own line on the sender's statement. The recipient still receives the full amount.\n\n## User Stories\n\n1. As a sender, I want a transfer to cost me the amount plus the fee, so that the recipient receives the full amount.\n2. As finance, I want the fee to be 1% of the amount, rounded half up to the cent and never less than one cent, so that every transfer pays something and rounding is predictable.\n3. As a sender, I want a transfer I cannot pay together with its fee to be rejected with nothing changed, so that I never end up below zero or half charged.\n4. As a sender, I want the fee as its own line on my statement right after the transfer, so that I can see what the transfer cost.\n5. As a recipient, I want my statement to show only the amount I received, so that the sender's fee is not my concern.\n6. As finance, I want the fees collected in one fee account and the total money in the system unchanged by a transfer, so that the books balance.\n7. As an integrator, I want the existing functions and their return values unchanged, so that callers keep working.\n\n## Implementation Decisions\n\n- One seam: the public functions of the wallet and statement modules.\n- A new module `src/fees.js` exports `quoteFee(cents)`, the fee in cents for an amount in cents, computed with integer arithmetic.\n- The fee account is `sys:fees`; it is opened the first time a fee is charged.\n- A transfer posts all of its ledger entries in one posting: the sender's `transfer-out`, then the sender's fee with kind `fee`, the recipient's `transfer-in`, and the fee account's income. Each carries the transfer identifier as `ref`.\n- A transfer is rejected with `INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS` when the balance is below the amount plus the fee, before anything is posted.\n- `transfer(from, to, cents)` still returns the transfer identifier; `deposit`, `balance`, `statement`, `formatStatement` and the ledger's exports keep their signatures.\n- **Security:** assets are the account balances and the fee income. Threats: a transfer that overdraws by the fee (tampering with the balance rule), a fee lost or doubled by a partial posting (integrity), a fee hidden inside the amount (repudiation). Controls: funds are checked against amount plus fee, one posting per transfer, the fee is its own ledger entry and statement line.\n\n## Testing Decisions\n\n- Test through the wallet and statement modules' public functions; assert balances, statement lines and error codes.\n- Good tests: boundary amounts for the rounding (50, 149, 150 and 250 cents), a transfer of exactly balance minus fee, a rejected transfer leaving the ledger untouched.\n- Prior art: the existing wallet and statement tests, which must be updated where the fee changes a balance.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Fee exemptions, tiers or configurable rates.\n- Refunding a fee.\n- Fees on deposits.\n\n## Further Notes\n\n- Amounts are integer cents everywhere; only the money module knows decimals.\n"}
# Migration: the data file moves from decimal units to integer cents; existing files convert on load with a backup,
# newer or corrupt files are refused untouched. Hidden checks run on scratch files with real float noise.
{"id": "L5-storage-migration", "fixture": "migration", "hidden": "hidden_migration.mjs", "pass_score": 0.67, "hidden_checks": ["migrates_v1", "backup_kept", "idempotent", "rejects_future_version", "rejects_corrupt", "missing_file_is_empty", "api_in_cents"], "prompt": "/aegisops-harness:aidlc Build this spec and take it all the way to a change that is ready to ship. I am not available for questions, so make reasonable decisions and write them down.\n\nStatus: ready-for-agent\n\n# Wallet storage in cents\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nThe wallet stores balances in its data file as decimal numbers of currency units. The file already holds values such as 0.30000000000000004, and sums drift by fractions of a cent. Existing files must keep working.\n\n## Solution\n\nThe data file moves to version 2: integer cents and a version field. A version 1 file is converted the first time it is loaded, the original is kept beside it, and the wallet works in cents from then on.\n\n## User Stories\n\n1. As an operator, I want balances stored and handled as integer cents, so that sums are exact.\n2. As an operator, I want a version 1 file converted to cents when it is loaded, so that existing data keeps working without a manual step.\n3. As an operator, I want the original version 1 file kept beside the new one before the first write, so that I can roll back.\n4. As an operator, I want loading a version 2 file to change nothing on disk, so that the conversion runs once.\n5. As an operator, I want a file with a newer version refused untouched, so that an old build cannot damage newer data.\n6. As an operator, I want a version 1 file with a balance that is not a non-negative number refused untouched, so that bad data is not converted silently.\n7. As a developer, I want a missing file to load as an empty state without creating it, so that a fresh install starts clean.\n\n## Implementation Decisions\n\n- One seam for storage: `src/store.js` exports `load(path)`, `save(path, state)` and a pure `migrate(raw)`.\n- Version 2 file: `{ \"version\": 2, \"balances\": { \"<user>\": <integer cents> } }`. A file with no `version` field is version 1.\n- `migrate` converts each version 1 amount with `Math.round(amount * 100)` and returns a version 2 state; a version 2 input is returned as it is.\n- `load` of a version 1 file first copies the original bytes to `<name>.v1.bak.json` in the same folder (`wallet.json` becomes `wallet.v1.bak.json`; an existing backup is never overwritten), then writes the version 2 file in its place.\n- A `version` other than 2 raises an error with code `UNSUPPORTED_VERSION`; a version 1 balance that is not a finite number of at least 0 raises `CORRUPT_DATA`. Neither writes any file.\n- A missing file loads as `{ \"version\": 2, \"balances\": {} }` and is not created until the first save.\n- The wallet module takes and returns integer cents: `deposit(user, cents)`, `transfer(from, to, cents)`, `balance(user)`. An amount that is not a positive integer raises `INVALID_AMOUNT`. `WALLET_FILE` still selects the data file.\n- **Security:** assets are the stored balances. Threats: money created or lost by the conversion (tampering), a newer or corrupt file overwritten (integrity), no way back after a bad conversion (availability). Controls: one fixed rounding rule, refuse and leave untouched for unknown versions and bad balances, a backup written before the first write.\n\n## Testing Decisions\n\n- Test the store through `load`, `save` and `migrate` on scratch files, and the wallet through its public functions with `WALLET_FILE` pointing at a scratch file.\n- Good tests: the three amounts in `data/wallet.json` convert to 30, 1999 and 100 cents; a second load leaves the file byte for byte unchanged; a refused file is unchanged and has no backup.\n- Prior art: the existing store and wallet tests, which must move to cents.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Converting the repository's own `data/wallet.json` by hand: it converts on its first load.\n- Several currencies.\n- Concurrent writers.\n\n## Further Notes\n\n- The existing tests point the wallet at a scratch file through `WALLET_FILE` before importing it.\n"}
# Concurrency: read-check-write over an asynchronous store. The fixture's sequential tests are green while 50
# concurrent transfers overdraw. Hidden checks: no overdraft, no lost update, no deadlock, no global lock.
{"id": "L6-concurrent-transfers", "fixture": "concurrency", "hidden": "hidden_concurrency.mjs", "pass_score": 0.67, "hidden_checks": ["sequential_still_works", "no_overdraft", "no_lost_deposit", "no_deadlock", "rejected_writes_nothing", "independent_wallets_overlap"], "prompt": "/aegisops-harness:aidlc Build this spec and take it all the way to a change that is ready to ship. I am not available for questions, so make reasonable decisions and write them down.\n\nStatus: ready-for-agent\n\n# Safe concurrent transfers\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nThe wallet reads a balance, waits for the store, and writes the new balance. Two transfers issued at the same time from one wallet both pass the balance check, and deposits issued together overwrite each other. The existing tests run one operation at a time and do not see it.\n\n## Solution\n\nOperations on the same wallet run one after another; operations on unrelated wallets still run side by side. A transfer never leaves a wallet below zero and no update is lost.\n\n## User Stories\n\n1. As a wallet owner, I want transfers issued together from my wallet never to take it below zero, so that I cannot be overdrawn.\n2. As a wallet owner, I want deposits issued together to all count, so that no money is lost.\n3. As a wallet owner, I want two transfers in opposite directions between the same wallets to both finish, so that the service never hangs.\n4. As an operator, I want transfers between unrelated wallets not to wait for each other, so that one busy wallet does not slow everyone down.\n5. As a wallet owner, I want a rejected transfer to change no balance, so that a failure has no side effects.\n6. As an integrator, I want the functions, their results and their error codes unchanged, so that callers keep working.\n\n## Implementation Decisions\n\n- One seam: the wallet module's async functions `deposit(user, cents)`, `transfer(from, to, cents)` and `balance(user)`.\n- Mutual exclusion per wallet, held for the whole read, check and write of an operation. A transfer holds the locks of both wallets and always takes them in the same order (sorted wallet identifiers).\n- No single lock for all wallets.\n- `src/db.js` is the storage interface and stays as it is.\n- Of N transfers of the same amount issued together, exactly as many succeed as the balance covers; the rest are rejected with `INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS`.\n- **Security:** assets are the balances. Threats: a double spend through two concurrent transfers (tampering), a lost deposit (integrity), a deadlock between opposite transfers (denial of service). Controls: per-wallet mutual exclusion around read, check and write, a fixed lock order, the balance check inside the lock.\n\n## Testing Decisions\n\n- Test through the wallet module's public functions with operations started together (`Promise.all`, `Promise.allSettled`); assert balances and how many succeeded.\n- Good tests: 20 transfers of 1 from a balance of 5 give exactly 5 successes; 30 deposits of 1 give 30; opposite transfers both finish.\n- Prior art: the existing wallet tests.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Several processes or machines sharing one store.\n- Changing the store's interface or latency.\n- Retrying rejected transfers.\n\n## Further Notes\n\n- The store answers every call asynchronously, as a remote database would.\n"}
